{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1784150897,"nonce":"zurUCGKdHiZeF8zRhBC7pMRc9D8=","title":"Environment `allowed_refs` protection (ADR-0037) is checked against `config_ref(event)`, which returns the **commit SHA** for Push/PullRequest/Manual/Api events (GitHub's `after`/`head`; a dispatch's resolved SHA), not the branch ref. `ProtectionRules::ref_allowed` glob-matches that value, so a branch-pattern rule (e.g. `refs/heads/main` or `main`) can **never** match a SHA — allowed-refs is effectively a no-op for these event kinds (only Tag/Release feed a `refs/tags/*` string).","message":"Pre-existing (predates ADR-0043; affects the webhook push/PR path). Surfaced while verifying ADR-0043 slice 2, which faithfully mirrors the webhook behavior — so dispatch inherits the same gap. It matters most for manual dispatch, whose headline case (deploy-to-prod-on-a-button) is exactly what allowed-refs is meant to gate.\n\nLikely fix: separate the \"ref to read config at\" (SHA, immutable — correct as-is) from the \"ref to evaluate allowed-refs against\" (the branch/tag ref). Needs a small decision on ref semantics across push/PR/tag/dispatch — arguably an ADR-0037 refinement. For dispatch specifically, the user-supplied branch ref (currently discarded in favour of the resolved SHA) would be the value to check.\n\n- [ ] `ref_allowed` is evaluated against the branch/tag ref, not the commit SHA, for Push/PR/Manual/Api.\n- [ ] A branch-pattern allowed-refs rule correctly admits/rejects a push and a dispatch on that branch.\n- [ ] Config is still read + the run still pinned at the immutable SHA (unchanged).\n- [ ] Regression test covering both a webhook push and a manual dispatch against a branch-scoped Environment.\n\nNone - can start immediately (independent of the ADR-0043 UI slices).","files":null}]}